WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.800
What has fans complaining the most about&nbsp;
"Wednesday" on Netflix? It's not the acting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.580
or the story. It's a common problem&nbsp;
that a lot of streaming series have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.400
been running into – and it looks ugly, literally.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.780
Streamers have had a rough go of it lately&nbsp;
when it comes to fan reception to CGI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:20.940
on a streaming-TV budget. The initial&nbsp;
responses to the CGI in the "She-Hulk:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.500
Attorney at Law" trailer were so intense, for&nbsp;
example, that Disney did its best to quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.720
and quietly improve it between the time that the&nbsp;
YouTube trailer dropped and the series debuted.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.800
Now, Netflix is under fire for&nbsp;
the CGI in its new macabre series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.190
"Wednesday," based on the deadpan teenage&nbsp;
daughter from "The Addams Family."

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:41.040
"I'm failing to see the problem here."

00:00:41.278 --> 00:00:46.620
OK, we'll explain. The focus of the criticism&nbsp;
for the Tim Burton-directed series has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:52.020
been on the monster called a Hyde. It's very&nbsp;
Burton-esque, with its large eyes and sharp teeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:57.180
but far from realistic. The "Addams Family"&nbsp;
movies from the 1990s were a bit campy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:02.220
so they weren't known for realistic special&nbsp;
effects. But Netflix's "Wednesday" is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.240
dark and more grown-up tale of murder&nbsp;
and prejudice. Its themes are heavier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.680
and fans seem to be disappointed that the CGI&nbsp;
didn't live up to that standard of realism.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.460
Plenty of fans have taken to Twitter&nbsp;
to voice their frustration about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.000
show's effects. One even said they&nbsp;
were fighting the urge to turn off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.200
the show because they found the CGI&nbsp;
so grating. Another begged Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.800
to increase the show's budget so that&nbsp;
they wouldn't have to stop watching.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.080
Other viewers were amused by what they see as the&nbsp;
low quality of the digital animation. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.220
one admitted to giggling at how awful it&nbsp;
was, but still punctuated their tweet with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.780
a skull emoji to express their disapproval.&nbsp;
Another wondered if the special effects were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.340
"a goof," some shared poorly animated gifs to&nbsp;
show what they thought the effects looked like.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.840
It wasn't all joking around, though.&nbsp;
One viewer suggested an alternative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.460
animation method, one that Burton has been&nbsp;
associated with in the past, tweeting,

00:02:06.778 --> 00:02:11.100
It seems that some fans would have preferred&nbsp;
that the monster be made with Tim Burton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.660
unique stop-motion style from previous&nbsp;
projects like "The Corpse Bride" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.400
"Beetlejuice" rather than trying to split&nbsp;
the difference between quirky and realistic.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.840
Thankfully, there is one visual effect that&nbsp;
most fans agree couldn't have turned out better:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:30.960
Thing, as portrayed by Victor Dorobantu. The&nbsp;
disembodied hand follows Wednesday around for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.100
the bulk of her journey, helping her as she&nbsp;
unravels the mystery behind Nevermore Academy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:40.920
and its many strange characters. All the while,&nbsp;
the visual trickery is incredibly convincing.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:46.860
The official Netflix Twitter account dropped some&nbsp;
behind-the-scenes Thing photos featuring Dorobantu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:51.960
bringing his beloved character to life wearing&nbsp;
a blue suit so he could be digitally removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:58.140
in the edit. Many fans took this opportunity to&nbsp;
shower Dorobantu and Thing with love. One wrote,

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.524
"Oh, Thing. You poor, naive appendage."

00:03:09.524 --> 00:03:13.500
Another described Thing as the perfect&nbsp;
blend of visual and practical effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.420
and one fan even admitted they were totally duped&nbsp;
by how the "Wednesday" team made Thing happen.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.440
When it came to removing Dorobantu's&nbsp;
body from the scenes he appears in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:27.600
the VFX team did a fine job. But when it&nbsp;
came time to construct an entire Hyde,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.600
many fans seem to agree that&nbsp;
they just missed the mark.

